>> A2: Briefly describe what happens in a call to timer_sleep(),
>> including the effects of the timer interrupt handler.

1. timer_sleep() returns immediately if ticks <= 0. While interrups inactive briefly, it sets the current thread's wkup_ticks to start+ticks and inserts the thread into sleep_list in sorted order. Interrupts are enabled and sema_down() is called on its own sleep_sema which blocks the thread.

2. The timer interrupt handler increment ticks (per tick) and then looks at the end of sleep_list (for the lowest value wkup_ticks). While the specific threads wkup_ticks is <= ticks, it removes thread from the list and calls sema_up() on its sleep_sema, and is put on the ready list after unblocking the thread. It stops at the frist thread that requires waiting.

>> A3: What steps are taken to minimize the amount of time spent in
>> the timer interrupt handler?

1. The handler never scans the wholes list as sleep_list is always kept sorted. It only the checks the last element (the first to wake up thread) and wakes it if "wake up" time has arrive, and repeats until it stops at a thread whose wkup_ticks is higher than current ticks.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> A4: How are race conditions avoided when multiple threads call
>> timer_sleep() simultaneously?
seep_list is shared with the timer interrupt handler, which cannot acquire locks, so interrupts are disabled during insertion into sleep_list. So interrupts is disable during insertion into sleep_list. During this window, no other thread can run on a single CPU. Two threads can not modify sleep_list at the same time. Interrupts are off only for the list insertion. 

>> A5: How are race conditions avoided when a timer interrupt occurs
>> during a call to timer_sleep()?

he list insertion happens with interrupts off, so the handler never sees sleep_list half updated. If the interrupt fires after interrupts turns on again but before sema_down(), the handlers sema_up() still counts. Tthe semaphores value becomes 1, so sema_down() returns right away and the wake up is nott lost.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> A6: Why did you choose this design?  In what ways is it superior to
>> another design you considered?

Blocking on a semaphore uses no CPU while the thread waits, so theres no busy waiting. It also keeps interrupts off only for the list insertion, and the semaphore handles the wake-up race in A5 without extra code. We keep sleep_list sorted so the interrupt handler, instead of checking every sleeping thread each tick, it only checks ones that are due.


